@FOOTNOTE{Note1,key="Note1",note="The standard Wilks asymptotic result $\Delta \chi ^2\sim \chi ^2(1)$ for one extra parameter assumes the maximum-likelihood estimator lies in the \protect \emph  {interior} of the parameter space; here the physical constraint $r_{\protect \rm  foam,0}\geq 0$ places the best fit on the boundary, so this assumption fails. The Chernoff (1954) boundary theorem~\cite {Chernoff1954} replaces it: when the true value of a non-negative parameter lies on the boundary, the asymptotic distribution of the likelihood-ratio statistic $\Delta \chi ^2$ is a 50:50 mixture of a point mass at zero and a $\chi ^2(1)$ distribution. Heuristically, half the realizations of the noise drive the unconstrained estimator above zero (contributing the $\chi ^2(1)$ piece), and half drive it below zero where the constraint pins $r_{\protect \rm  foam,0}=0$ exactly (the point mass). The 95th percentile of the mixture therefore coincides with the 90th percentile of $\chi ^2(1)$, $\Delta \chi ^2=2.71$ --- identical to the standard one-sided Gaussian $95\%$ CL threshold, but derived for the correct boundary-constrained sampling distribution."}
